## Chemicals and pesticides

### Carbon monoxide

### Organophosphorus insecticides and nerve agents

- **Organophosphorus compounds:**
  - Commonly found in pesticides and insecticides
  - Ocassionally found in nerve agents (chemical warfare; G refers to Germany while V refers to venomous)

  
  ![](img/OP_compounds.png)
- **Mechanism of toxicity of OP compounds:**
  - <u>Inactivation of AChE</u> - through phosphonylation of the active site of acetyl-cholinesterase resulting in accumulation of ACh in cholinergic synapse
  - <u>Ageing of AChE enzyme and permanent loss</u> - normally, spontaneous hydrolysis results in reactivation of enzyme, but loss of a chemical group prevents further enzyme reactivation (N.B. rate of ageing is more rapid w/ nerve agents \[instantaneous\] dimethyl compouds \[3h\] vs diethyl compounds \[31h\]) 
  ![](img/mechanism_of_toxicity_of_OP.png)
- **Clinical features of OP poisoning:**
  - **Acute chlinergic syndrome** (cholinergic crisis) - lasts 48-72h:
    - <u>Increased parasympathetic tone</u>:
      - Miosis and blurred vision, w/ lacrimation
      - Increased GI motility (N/V and diarrhoea) and increased salivation
      - Bradycardia
      - Urinary urgency and frequency with incontinence
    - <u>Increased nicotinic receptor stimulation</u> - fasciculation followed by flaccid paralysis

    
    ![](img/acute_cholinergic_syndrome.png)
  - **The intermediate syndrome** (20%) - syndrome characterised by progressive weakness 1-4d after exposure spreading from 1) occular muscles, 2) bulbar muscles, and 3) respiratory muscles, lasting for 2-3 weeks
  - **Organophosphate-induced delayed polyneuropathy** (OPIDN) - occurs 2-3 weeks after acute exposure, usually a mixed sensory/motor distal neuropathy, where recovery is often incomplete and takes years
- **Ix:**
  - <u>Plasma or RBC cholinesterase</u> - poorly correlates w/ severity (\< 10% indicates severe poisoning)
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Primary assessment and immediate Mx</u> - e.g. cleared airway secretions, Mx of airway, high-flow O2, and IV access
    - <u>Prevention of further exposure</u> - removal of contaminated cloathing, contact lens, and wash w/ soap and water (gastric lavage/ activated charcoal if ingested)
    - <u>Cholinergic antagonism</u> - sufficient doses of atropine in severe cases (+/- oxime for reactivation of AChE)\*\*\* Carbamate insecticides

### Methanol and ethylene glycol

- **Definition** - related poisoning agents as both are seen in antifreeze, breake fluids, and windscreen washes
- **Pathophysiology of methanol and ethylene glycol poisoing** - both are not intrinsically toxic substances:
  - <u>Metabolism of ethylene glycol and methanol</u> - converted by alcoholic dehydrogenase into metabolically toxic substances 
  ![](img/metabolism_of_methanol_and_ethylene_glycol.png)
- **Clinical features of ethylene glycol poisoning:**
  - **Early features of methanol or ethylene glycol poisoning:**
    - <u>CNS manifestations</u> - ataxia, bulbar Sx, drowsiness, nystagmus, seizures
    - <u>Metabolic manifestations</u> - metabolic acidosis (and accompanying tachypnoea)
  - **Toxic effects of ethylene glycol:**
    - <u>Cranial nerve palsies</u> - opthalmoplegia is the predominant feature, although other CN palsies are possible
    - <u>Flaccid paralysis</u> - characterised by hyporeflexia
    - <u>Myoclonus</u> - sudden brief jerks on background of hyporeflexia
    - <u>Acute tubular necrosis</u> - caused by precipitation of calcium oxalate in kidneys, often w/ concomitent renal pain, hypocalcaemia, hyperkalaemia, and hypomagnesemia
  - **Toxic effects of methanol poisoning:**
    - <u>Visual Sx</u> - visual impairments (blindness), photophobia with impaired visual reflexes caused by optic disc and retinal oedema
    - <u>Non-specific Sx</u> - headache, confusion, vertigo
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Antidote</u> - ethanol or fomepizole administered on clinical suspicion to delay formation of toxic metabolites
    - <u>Supportive care</u> - e.g. benzodiazepine for seizures, HD for severe renal failure

